        "Air pollution control"^^xsd:string,
        "Fluid mechanics"^^xsd:string,
        "Particle technology"^^xsd:string ;
    dcterms:title "Smoke, Dust, and Haze: Fundamentals of Aerosol Dynamics"^^xsd:string ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://w3id.org/emmo/disciplines/statesofmatter#> .

emmo:EMMO_04f2a2d5_e799_4692_a654_420e76f5acc1 a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "GaseousState"@en ;
    rdfs:comment """A gas is a state of matter in which particles (e.g., atoms or molecules) have enough kinetic energy to overcome the intermolecular forces binding them together, so they move freely and independently, filling whatever container holds them. Unlike a liquid, a gas has no definite volume and is readily compressible.
A gaseous state can be categorised univocally whether the substance sits below or above critical temperature T𞁞​, because that alone determines whether a liquid–vapor phase boundary exists. Below T𞁞​ the gaseous phase is condensable by isothermal compression; at and above T𞁞 no amount of pressure produces a distinct liquid.
A gas differs from a plasma in that its particles remain electrically neutral and un-ionized; once a significant fraction of particles ionize, the system becomes a plasma, which responds to electromagnetic fields collectively."""@en,
        "A gaseous state is a compressible fluid, a state of matter that has no fixed shape and no fixed volume."@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://w3id.org/emmo/disciplines/statesofmatter#> ;
    rdfs:seeAlso <urn:isbn:9780471400653>,
        <urn:isbn:9780521408448> ;
    rdfs:subClassOf emmo:EMMO_4e9fd152_147b_4564_8999_089608f41aff,
        emmo:EMMO_87ac88ff_8379_4f5a_8c7b_424a8fff1ee8,
        emmo:EMMO_fc9222ed_14af_419b_b9df_b6a8a04e9d38 ;
    skos:altLabel "Gas"@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "GaseousState"@en ;
    emmo:OWLRL2DLAxioms "Disjoint Union Of: SupercriticalGaseousState, Vapor"@en ;
    emmo:conceptualisation """A gas is a state of matter in which particles (e.g., atoms or molecules) have enough kinetic energy to overcome the intermolecular forces binding them together, so they move freely and independently, filling whatever container holds them. Unlike a liquid, a gas has no definite volume and is readily compressible.
A gaseous state can be categorised univocally whether the substance sits below or above critical temperature T𞁞​, because that alone determines whether a liquid–vapor phase boundary exists. Below T𞁞​ the gaseous phase is condensable by isothermal compression; at and above T𞁞 no amount of pressure produces a distinct liquid.
A gas differs from a plasma in that its particles remain electrically neutral and un-ionized; once a significant fraction of particles ionize, the system becomes a plasma, which responds to electromagnetic fields collectively."""@en ;
    emmo:elucidation "A gaseous state is a compressible fluid, a state of matter that has no fixed shape and no fixed volume."@en ;
    emmo:note "\"Vapor\" and \"gas\" are often used interchangeably in casual speech, but in the strict sense they are different branches of the tree, divided by T𞁞. A vapor can be liquefied by pressure alone; a gas cannot. This is why \"water vapor\" is correct (water's Tc​ = 647 K is far above ambient) while room-temperature N₂ or O₂ is properly a gas."@en ;
    emmo:wikipediaReference <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gas> .

emmo:EMMO_0527413c_b286_4e9c_b2d0_03fb2a038dee a owl:Class ;
    rdfs:label "Interpreter"@en ;
    rdfs:comment "The entity (or agent, or observer, or cognitive entity) who connects 'Sign', 'Interpretant' and 'Object'."@en ;
    rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://w3id.org/emmo/disciplines/properties#> ;
    rdfs:subClassOf [ a owl:Restriction ;
            owl:onProperty [ owl:inverseOf emmo:EMMO_cb804456_9459_4be5_ac63_a291a872fbd0 ] ;
            owl:someValuesFrom emmo:EMMO_008fd3b2_4013_451f_8827_52bceab11841 ],
        [ a owl:Restriction ;
            owl:onProperty emmo:EMMO_cd9a3a58_dabc_4c46_9ea4_03ca000b2022 ;
            owl:someValuesFrom emmo:EMMO_b21a56ed_f969_4612_a6ec_cb7766f7f31d ],
        [ a owl:Restriction ;
            owl:onProperty emmo:EMMO_da27d905_beb6_44ea_a330_a2dd9c17b263 ;
            owl:someValuesFrom emmo:EMMO_054af807_85cd_4a13_8eba_119dfdaaf38b ],
        [ a owl:Restriction ;
            owl:onProperty emmo:EMMO_f2fc1ce9_cc3b_4eb5_a112_3c85d1b1374a ;
            owl:someValuesFrom emmo:EMMO_6f5af708_f825_4feb_a0d1_a8d813d3022b ],
        emmo:EMMO_b803f122_4acb_4064_9d71_c1e5fd091fc9 ;
    skos:example """For example, the ontologist may be interest in cataloguing in the EMMO how the same object (e.g. a cat) is addressed using different signs (e.g. cat, gatto, chat) by different interpreters (e.g. english, italian or french people).

The same applies for the results of measurements: the ontologist may be interest to represent in the EMMO how different measurement processes (i.e. semiosis) lead to different quantitative results (i.e. signs) according to different measurement devices (i.e. interpreters)."""@en ;
    skos:prefLabel "Interpreter"@en ;
    emmo:OWLRL2DLAxioms "EquivalentTo. Cogniser or Declarer or Deducer"^^xsd:string ;
    emmo:elucidation "The entity (or agent, or observer, or cognitive entity) who connects 'Sign', 'Interpretant' and 'Object'."@en ;
    emmo:note """The interpreter is not the ontologist, being the ontologist acting outside the ontology at the meta-ontology level.

On the contrary, the interpreter is an agent recognized by the ontologist. The semiotic branch of the EMMO is the tool used by the ontologist to represent an interpreter's semiotic activity."""@en .

